WEBVTT

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:04.350
PAUL USHER: Welcome to this
Microsoft build 2026 session.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.780
I'm Paul Usher from DevExpress.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.990
Today we're looking at how AI
can create a more natural user

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:13.380
experience inside a modern
Blazor application.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.780
This is not about generating
code and it's not

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.330
about dropping a chat bot
beside an existing app

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:19.830
and calling it done.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:23.370
This is about connecting AI
to the controls, to the data,

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:27.150
and to the workflows
users already rely on.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.810
The demo app is built in
Visual Studio using.NET.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:34.470
DevExpress Blazor controls,
DevExpress reporting,

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:38.790
and Azure Open AI
through iChat client.

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:41.130
We'll look at three examples.

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:46.140
First how AI drives DevExpress
grid using tool calling.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.530
Second AI is built in to the
DevExpress report viewer

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.090
for translation.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:54.630
And third Azure Open AI
to review a contract

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:58.620
and DevExpress report to
visualize the risky clauses.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:03.030
We have a simple command center
here featuring some KPI cards,

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:06.450
a DX chart control, DX grid,

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:10.500
and on the right-hand
side DX AI chat.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.590
I've got some prebaked
recording prompts.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:17.130
So we're starting on the
main home razor page.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.800
The cell's data is
coming from a CSV loaded

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.570
through a data service

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:24.150
and a simple method
call to get orders.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:27.510
It's also using a get
summary for the KPI cards

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:30.990
and a get region
metrics for the chart.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.340
The grid itself is bound

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.850
to around 10,000 rows
of USA sales data.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:39.540
Now traditionally users will
work with data by filtering,

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.210
sorting, grouping,
paging, and exporting.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:45.270
And that still matters and the
DevExpress grid is very good

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:46.800
at that.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.230
Let's filter to just Texas.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:54.580
And then maybe group by region.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.900
Expand. And sort
by customer name.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:04.980
We could also create some
buttons that will build

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.530
that functionality in.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:11.110
They only cover the scenarios
we thought ahead of time.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.210
So the question is can we give
the user a more natural way

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:17.320
to work with this data.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:25.260
Well, on the right-hand side
I've got a DX AI chat control.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:26.790
And what (inaudible)
here is the fact

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:30.300
that the user can ask questions
in a more natural language.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:34.710
For example, how did
we perform this week?

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.000
The prompt is going
to be handled

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.160
by the cell's analysis tool.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.490
It can call methods such
as summarize performance

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:44.550
which uses a build
executive summary method

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.670
and produces a concise
business summary.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:50.790
What if we want the chat to
actually interact with the grid?

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:54.250
Let's ask, "Group the
orders by customer."

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.250
And we can see that the
grid has now responded

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:02.380
and is grouping the
data by customer.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:05.790
What if I was to
actually say instead

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:09.030
of customer group by profit?

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:11.190
And we can see that the
code behind has been set

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.770
up to allow specific
functionality.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:15.900
The user doesn't
just get told no.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.470
It's provided an explanation
as to what's going on.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:24.060
Maybe now I ask it to highlight
the payment risk accounts.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.990
And again that grid
information is updated.

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:31.560
In the background it's calling
a filter risk account method.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:34.200
Now that I've got that
information I want

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.270
to export it to Excel.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:39.160
So just ask export.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.070
And we can see now that a
download's been created

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:47.100
with an Excel SX file.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.320
This is the difference
between a chat answer

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.210
and an application assistant.

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:54.600
The AI is not telling
the user how to export.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.320
It's triggering the exports for
a controlled DevExpress API.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.560
Think in terms of the
AI handles the intent.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.580
DevExpress handles
the interaction.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.950
Jumping in to Visual
Studio we'll take a peek

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:10.240
inside home.razor.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.430
First thing we want to do
is find the chat control.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:20.400
This is the DX AI chat
control for the page.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:23.940
The important setting is the
chat client service key equals

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:25.050
DX tools.

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:26.520
This is telling the chat control

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:28.500
to use the tool
enabled AI client

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:31.980
that we register in program.CS.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:34.470
I've said include function
calling for to true

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:37.650
so the demo can show the calls
the tool actually makes.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:40.900
Typically in production
you'd set it to false.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:43.410
Now if we scroll down to the

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:49.410
on after render we
can see the creation

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:52.680
of a new AI tools
context builder.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:55.380
And this context registers
two live targets,

00:04:55.380 --> 00:05:00.390
the actual DX grid instance
and the cell's data service.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:03.600
Then it registers the methods
that the AI is allowed to call.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:04.710
Filter by region.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:05.970
Filter by state.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:07.380
Filter risk accounts.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.030
Group by. Clear view.

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:10.650
Export. Summarize.

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:13.620
And list top customers.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.770
Think of this as the toolbox
for the current screen.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:19.320
The AI does not get
the whole application,

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:23.490
simply the approved capabilities
that we register here.

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:26.900
And we can see at the bottom of
the method the context is added

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:29.510
to the AI tools container
which makes it available

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:33.450
to the DevExpress tool
calling pipeline.

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:38.310
Switching across to the cell's
grid AI tools we'll scroll

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:42.270
down to the filter
by region method.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:45.210
So this is just one of the
tools the model can call

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:48.930
and it's just normal static C
Sharp method with meta data.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:53.850
So the AI integration tool gives
the model the function name.

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:55.950
And then we've got
the description

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.200
which tells the model
when to use the function

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.300
and what the parameters mean.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:01.740
Now these descriptions matter

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:04.720
because they guide
the tool selection.

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:09.600
So this is the DevExpress
specific part.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.910
The model does not
provide the grid.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.790
DevExpress injects the
live DX grid instance

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:17.490
from the tool's
context at run time.

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:20.700
The body then calls the
normal DevExpress grid API.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:21.780
It's the same sort of code

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:24.480
that I could write
from a button click.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:30.480
And the rest of the tools follow
exactly the same behavior.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:32.610
Not every tool changes the UI.

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:36.090
Some of them provide
an analysis.

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.520
Jumping in to program
CS we can see

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:42.720
where the actual chat
client has been added.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:44.850
We can see the
configuration is picking

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:50.370
up from our.NET user secrets
the Azure Open AI end point,

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:54.310
the Azure Open AI key,
and the deployment name.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:59.290
This next element is an
important one for the grid demo.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:05.580
We can see that we're creating a
key chat client called DX tools.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:10.410
And then we use DX tools as the
DevExpress tool definitions.

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:13.320
Use function invocation
executes the tool calls

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:16.230
and feeds the results
back to the model.

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:19.230
And that's what's being
used by the DX AI chat back

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:22.050
on the home razor page.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:25.410
One of the key take aways here
is that this is the main loop.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:30.600
The user prompt Azure Open AI
DevExpress tool public control

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:33.870
API real UI action.

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:39.420
Let's jump back and look at some
more built in AI functionality.

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:42.900
This time we're going to jump
in to the report viewer.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:45.780
And what we can see here is a
generative report using the

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:47.700
DevExpress reporting tools

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:50.340
and it's actually a
quarterly memo report.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:54.120
The problem is it's
been written in French.

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:58.380
Using the built in AI tooling
I can select that I want

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:04.860
to translate the entire
document back to English.

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:07.710
I'll press the translate button.

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:10.530
Now note that there's no custom
chat (inaudible) on this page.

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:15.240
The DevExpress report viewer
owns the AI experience.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.060
Now the report itself is
a standard extra report.

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:20.130
It sets up for
French memo section

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:22.620
and builds a simple
report header,

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:26.070
detail banned footer
and page footer.

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:31.950
And if we jump back in to Visual
Studio and scroll down to

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:34.860
where the report
engine is initialized

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:37.500
so the AI behavior's
enabled in program CS

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:40.350
through the ad Blazor reporting
AI integration methods

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:42.390
and ad translation.

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.850
We can see that the configured
languages include English,

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:48.000
French, Spanish,
German, Japanese.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.270
The enable translation
method adds the translation

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:54.630
and enable inline translation
allows the translated content

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:58.980
to appear inside the
rendered report experience.

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:03.610
In this pattern the control
owns the AI experience.

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:08.610
So let's jump back to
the app and take a look

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:12.990
at where we can compose
our own workflow.

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:16.710
This time we're going to
go to contract review.

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:19.920
We've got a master service
agreement rendered inside the

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.730
DevExpress report viewer.

00:09:22.730 --> 00:09:26.660
So the goal here is not just
to ask AI for a tech summary.

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:31.010
We want Azure Open AI to
identify risky clauses

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:32.540
and then we want
the document itself

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:35.310
to show those risky clauses.

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:38.150
I've wired up a button

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:43.440
to ask the chat control
to review for issues.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:45.750
So the AI's now
reviewing the contract.

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:49.110
It's going to identify
risky or one sided clauses.

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:52.650
And the report renders
with visual warnings.

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:55.470
We can see the side
panel findings.

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:59.550
We can see the highlighted
clauses, a warning tag,

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:02.950
and the red border on
the left as a warning.

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:06.450
The answers are on
the right-hand side,

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:10.230
but the experience is
inside the document.

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:14.040
Back in Visual Studio we'll
scroll down and take a look

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:17.870
at the review async method.

00:10:17.870 --> 00:10:22.310
Inside review async we know that
there is no DX AI chat control.

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:26.160
The page injects the
iChat client directly.

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:28.020
The system prompt
asks the model to act

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:30.090
as a commercial
contract attorney

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:33.030
and the user prompt sends
the full contract text using

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:36.600
contractreport.getfulltext.

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:39.210
Chat client get respond
async sends the request

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:41.970
to the configured AI client.

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:46.280
And then the response is
stored for the findings panel.

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:49.430
The parse risky clause
numbers extracts references

00:10:49.430 --> 00:10:51.560
like clause four or clause nine

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:54.440
and then the page creates a
new contract report passing

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:56.730
in the risky clause numbers.

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:59.820
Finally render findings
formats the AI response

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:01.980
for the side panel by
preserving the line breaks

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:03.660
and bold headings.

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:04.950
So the key take away here is

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:07.800
that the report doesn't
know anything about AI.

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:11.310
It only knows whether
a clause is risky.

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:14.790
So a contract report contains
the 12 contract clauses

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:17.190
and clauses source.

00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:19.560
Get full text joins
those clauses

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:22.290
in to a plain text that's
sent to the model.

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:24.510
The constructor receives
the risky clause numbers

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:27.330
and projects each clause in
to the report data source

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:30.600
with a is risky set
to true or false.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:32.940
So the report doesn't
know anything about AI.

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:36.130
It only knows whether
each clause is risky.

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:41.280
And if the clause is risky it's
going to change the band color

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:43.530
and add the warning tag.

00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:45.570
AI creates the state.

00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:48.540
DevExpress presents the state.

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:49.410
If we take a quick look

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:55.560
at the program structure we've
got our component pages,

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:59.130
AI folder, report services,
and then the data.

00:11:59.130 --> 00:12:00.540
And what I like about
this structure is

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:03.750
that the AI layer doesn't
swallow the application.

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:06.120
The page still uses the
DevExpress controls

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:07.710
for the user experience.

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:10.560
The services still own
the business data.

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:14.610
And the AI tools expose
selected capabilities.

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:18.330
The report renders through the
DevExpress reporting APIs.

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:21.870
And the DevExpress controls
help make the AI useful

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:26.250
because they give the AI
somewhere structured to act.

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:28.860
A grid can filter,
group, sort, and export.

00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:33.660
A report viewer can translate
and render document content.

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:36.270
And a report can visualize
AI derived state

00:12:36.270 --> 00:12:38.790
through conditional formatting.

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:43.950
So the AI is powerful, but
the control gives it shape.

00:12:43.950 --> 00:12:48.150
Across three pages we've shown
practical ways to bring AI

00:12:48.150 --> 00:12:51.300
in to a DevExpress application.

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:54.360
On the demo page AI drives
the DevExpress grid

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:56.040
through tool calling.

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:58.890
On the report viewer page
AI is built directly

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:00.540
in to the control.

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:03.330
And on the contract review
page AI becomes part

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:04.860
of a custom workflow

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:08.760
and DevExpress reports
visualize the result.

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:12.930
The point is not add a
chat bot beside every app.

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:16.800
It's to let users express
intent and then use controls

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:20.950
such as the DevExpress tools to
turn that intent in to action.

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:25.680
Azure Open AI provides
the intelligence.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:28.320
DevExpress provides the
application service.

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:31.570
And Visual Studio
brings it all together.
